Kent Paul Dolan wrote:
> Even the author of hypertext calls the web "that
> which is hyperlinked"

Googling for "that which is hyperlinked" only gets one hit, and it's
unrelated to Tim Berners-Lee.  At any rate, even if he did use a phrase
like that at some point he's pretty clear in his FAQ that he considers
only the network of hypertext (and not other things linked to from it)
to be the Web.  See the quotes from his FAQ I posted earlier in the
thread.

As I also mentioned earlier, Google's first definition likewise limits
itself to HTML.  Other technical sources (e.g. the Free Online
Dictionary of Computing) concur.

If you want to include things like email, Usenet, gopher, ftp, etc as
part of the Web, you have to realize that you're using a non-stnadard
definition and that other people will be confused by your usage--and
they're not likely to use it themselves.

Now, there are certainly Web interfaces that allow you to send things
to, say, Usenet, and those interfaces are part of the Web.  That
doesn't make Usenet itself a part of the web, though, any more than the
existance of www.flowers.com makes delivering flowers a part of the web.



